\end{table}

% ==========================================
% IV. RESULTS
% ==========================================
\section{Results}

\subsection{Quantitative Sentiment Shift}
Table III shows a dramatic and statistically significant shift in student sentiment following the "Glass Box" intervention.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Mean Student Sentiment Scores (1=Negative, 5=Positive)}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Phase 1} & \textbf{Phase 2} & \textbf{Delta ($\Delta$)} \\
\midrule
Trust in System & 2.4 $\pm$ 1.1 & \textbf{4.1 $\pm$ 0.8} & +1.7 \\
Anxiety Level & 4.2 $\pm$ 0.9 & \textbf{1.8 $\pm$ 0.7} & -2.4 \\
Perceived Utility & 3.1 $\pm$ 1.2 & \textbf{4.5 $\pm$ 0.6} & +1.4 \\
Understanding of Data & 1.5 $\pm$ 0.5 & \textbf{4.8 $\pm$ 0.4} & +3.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\label{tab:results}
\end{table}

The most significant gain was in **"Understanding of Data" (+3.3 points)**. In Phase 1, students were unsure what data was being collected. In Phase 2, seeing the skeletal wireframes (Paper 3) allowed them to intuitively grasp that their facial identity was not being stored, directly dismantling the "Panopticon" fear. The drop in "Anxiety Level" (-2.4 points) correlates strongly with this increased understanding.

\subsection{Demographic Variance Analysis}
We analyzed the results for demographic variance to see if technical literacy influenced trust.
\begin{itemize}
    \item **CS vs. Electronics:** Computer Science students showed a slightly higher initial skepticism (Phase 1 Trust: 2.1 vs 2.6), likely due to greater awareness of surveillance risks. However, they also showed a sharper increase in trust during Phase 2 (Delta +1.9 vs +1.5), suggesting that tech-literate users are more responsive to verifiable transparency mechanisms like Blockchain audits.
    \item **Gender Dynamics:** Female participants reported significantly higher "Anxiety" scores in Phase 1 (4.6 vs 3.9), reflecting broader societal trends in surveillance vulnerability. In Phase 2, this gap narrowed significantly (1.9 vs 1.7), indicating that "Visible Privacy" is particularly effective at reassuring vulnerable populations.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Statistical Significance Testing}
To validate the significance of the observed shifts in sentiment, we performed a paired sample t-test comparing Phase 1 and Phase 2 scores for each construct. The results confirm that the improvements in Trust ($t(539) = 14.2, p < 0.001$) and Understanding ($t(539) = 21.5, p < 0.001$) are statistically robust and not due to random chance. Furthermore, a Cohen's d effect size analysis yielded $d=1.8$ for the "Understanding of Data" metric, indicating a "very large" effect of the transparency intervention. This quantitative rigor reinforces the qualitative findings, demonstrating that the "Glass Box" approach fundamentally alters user perception.

\subsection{The "Skeleton Effect"}
To understand *why* trust increased, we asked students to identify the features that most influenced their perception. Crucially, **82\% of students cited the "Skeleton View"** as the primary driver of their trust.

The sophisticated backend cryptography (Paper 8) and blockchain integrity checks were less impactful ($15\%$) because they are invisible to the end-user. This creates a compelling finding: **Visible Privacy > Mathematical Privacy** in social contexts. Users trust what they can see.

